  "dom_id": "step-q344708",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013500/assets/c2e43e711a8c3973e080da7ee0e547b3fdec20ba4536ff7f79f21e8e4683c493.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-1,10],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments and the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(7,0)$ with radius $2.$ \n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[1,9].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region consists of two right triangles, so we find the total area by adding their areas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,9].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9-1}\\cdot (6-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
